// Practice quiz: Generating a Spring Boot project and its structure
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/spring-boot-basics/generating-a-spring-boot-project-and-its-structure

::o1 choosing a project::When you generate a project with Spring Initializr, what does the artifact value become? {
=The name of the build output and project#Correct. The artifact names the Maven artifact, and usually the project folder.
~The port the server listens on#Not quite. The port is a runtime setting.
~The Java version#Not quite. Java version is its own choice.
~The list of dependencies#Not quite. Dependencies are chosen separately.
}

::o2 settings location::A new setting needs to reach application.properties. Which folder is it in? {
=src/main/resources#Correct. Files there end up on the classpath.
~src/main/java#Not quite. That folder is compiled Java sources.
~target/classes#Not quite. That is build output and gets overwritten.
~The project root#Not quite. Boot looks on the classpath by default for this file.
}

::o2 scanning boundary::A class annotated with @RestController is placed in a sibling package of the main class. What happens by default? {
=It is not found by component scanning#Correct. Default scanning covers the main class package and everything below.
~It is found, because all classes in the jar are scanned#Not quite. Scanning has a root package.
~The build fails#Not quite. The code compiles fine; it just is not registered.
~Boot moves it at startup#Not quite. Boot never moves classes.
}

::o3 proving health::What does the generated test mainly prove when you run the Maven test goal? {
=The application context loads#Correct. The generated test starts the context and fails if wiring breaks.
~Every endpoint returns 200#Not quite. The generated test has no endpoint checks.
~The server is reachable from the internet#Not quite. It does not check external reachability.
~The code has no bugs#Not quite. A single context load cannot show that.
}

::o3 find the port::Where do you look to confirm the port a Boot app actually started on? {
=The startup log line from the embedded server#Correct. That line reports the real port, including any override.
~The parent section of pom.xml#Not quite. It has nothing about the runtime port.
~The main class name#Not quite. A class name does not set the port.
~The test report#Not quite. Test reports do not describe a normal run.
}

